var definitions = ['<h2>C’est très bien!</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it, this) and "est" (is), which commonly occurs before a vowel sound in French. It stands for "this is" or "it is" when followed by an adjective, noun, or adverb.</div><div><strong>est</strong> Included in the contraction "C’est", this is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being of something or someone.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "very". It is used to enhance the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "well" or "good". As an adverb, it modifies verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, but can also be used as an exclamation to indicate that something is good or satisfactory.</div>','<h2>C’est difficile à faire.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (which means "it" or "this") and "est" (which is the third-person singular of the verb "être", which means "to be"). Together "c’est" translates to "it is" or "this is" in English.</div><div><strong>est</strong> As noted above, this is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It’s used here as part of the contraction "c’est".</div><div><strong>difficile</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "difficult". In this sentence, it describes the task that is being talked about.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition that can mean "to" in English. In this context, it is used before the verb "faire" to form the construction "difficile à faire", which means "difficult to do".</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is an infinitive verb which means "to do" or "to make" in English. In the sentence, it’s used to express a general action that is difficult.</div>','<h2>C’est amusant!</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (which means "it" or "this" in English) and is followed by "est". The apostrophe replaces the "e" in "ce" when followed by a vowel or mute h for ease of pronunciation. Here, "C’" represents the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject "C’" to the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>amusant</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "fun" or "amusing". Here, it’s used in the masculine singular form to qualify the subject of the sentence. If the subject were feminine, it would be "amusante".</div>','<h2>C’est ma journée de congé.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (meaning "this" or "it") and "est" (the third-person singular present tense form of "être", meaning "is"). Together, "C’est" means "It is" or "This is".</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for the first-person singular, which means "my". It indicates that the following noun "journée" belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a French noun that means "day" in the sense of "daytime" or the period of a day usually devoted to work or other activities, in this context, it refers to a full day.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in French. In this context, it means "of", indicating a relationship between "journée" (day) and "congé" (leave).</div><div><strong>congé</strong> This is a French noun meaning "leave" or "day off". It refers to time off from work or a duty.</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas une bonne histoire.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French meaning "this" or "it". It is often used to refer to something that has been previously mentioned or is about to be specified.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" (negative part) and "est" (third-person singular form of "être", meaning "to be"). This construction negates the verb "is", making the sentence negative.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word works with "ne" to create the negation in French, and translates to "not" in English. The full negation "ne...pas" wraps around the verb to negate the statement.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French for feminine singular nouns, corresponding to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine form of the adjective "bon", meaning "good". In this sentence, it modifies the feminine noun "histoire", agreeing in gender and number.</div><div><strong>histoire</strong> This is a feminine singular noun meaning "story" or "history".</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas de ma faute.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, often translated as "this" or "that" in English. Here, it refers to a general situation or matter being discussed.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" (a negation particle) and "est" (third-person singular present tense of the verb "être", meaning "to be"). When combined as "n’est", it negates the verb, roughly translating to "is not" in English.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word works together with "ne" to form the negative construction in French, which is "ne... pas". It negates a statement and can be translated as "not".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition has multiple functions, and in this context, it is used to assign blame or responsibility, translating to "of" or "from". In this phrase, "de" can be understood to be part of an expression implying causation or blame, akin to saying "it’s not my fault" in English.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the first-person singular, meaning "my" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "faute" in this case.</div><div><strong>faute</strong> This noun translates to "fault" or "mistake" in English. When someone says "c’est ma faute", they’re admitting it’s their fault, but with "n’est pas", the speaker is denying responsibility.</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas très confortable.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is the demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "this" or "it". Here, it is the subject of the sentence and refers to an implied thing or situation.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" and "est", which forms part of the negation in French. "Est" is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". "Ne" is the negative particle used before the verb. Together, "n’est" negates the verb "to be".</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French, which typically follows the verb. When paired with "ne" (or its contraction form before a vowel, such as "n’est"), it translates to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very". It is used to modify the adjective that follows to increase its intensity.</div><div><strong>confortable</strong> This is an adjective meaning "comfortable". In this sentence, it is used in its negative form ("not very comfortable") to describe the subject "ce".</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas le tien!</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "this" or "it". It refers to a noun that has been previously mentioned or is obvious from context.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" (negative particle) and "est" (third-person singular present tense of "être", meaning "to be"). In combination, "n’est" negates the sentence and translates as "is not" in English.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negative construction in French. Together with "ne/n’", it negates a verb. "ne...pas" translates to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It corresponds to the singular masculine form.</div><div><strong>tien</strong> This is a possessive pronoun in French that means "yours". It is used to indicate possession and agrees in gender and number with the noun it replaces. Here, "le tien" implies it is replacing a masculine singular noun.</div>','<h2>Est-ce un gros problème?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This is a contraction of "est-ce que", a French phrase used to form questions. It can be translated as "is it" or "is this" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite masculine singular article which means "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>gros</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "big" or "large". Here, it’s used to qualify the noun that follows, matching it in gender and number, which in this sentence is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>problème?</strong> This is a French noun meaning "problem". It is masculine and singular in form. The question mark indicates that this word is part of a question being asked.</div>','<h2>Est-ce le bon numéro de téléphone?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This is a contraction of "est-ce que", a French phrase used to introduce a yes/no question. It can be translated as "Is it" or "Is this" in English.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is an adjective that means "good" or "correct" in English. In the context of this sentence, it is asking if something is correct.</div><div><strong>numéro</strong> This is a masculine noun that means "number" in English. Here, it refers to a sequence of digits assigned to a telephone.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that can be translated to many English words depending on the context, such as "of" or "from". In this case, it’s used to link "numéro" to "téléphone", forming the phrase "telephone number".</div><div><strong>téléphone?</strong> This is a masculine noun meaning "telephone" in English. When combined with "numéro", it creates the term "numéro de téléphone", which means "telephone number".</div>','<h2>Est-ce dangereux?</h2><div><strong>Est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It’s used here as part of the expression "est-ce que", which is a common way to form questions in French.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, meaning "this" or "it". In the question, it is part of the phrase "est-ce que", which leads the question and functions to turn the statement into a question form.</div><div><strong>dangereux?</strong> This is an adjective in French meaning "dangerous". It is used here to inquire about the safety of something. The word agrees in gender and number with the noun, but since the noun isn’t explicitly mentioned, it defaults to the masculine singular form.</div>','<h2>Est-ce froid?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This is a contraction of "est-ce que", which is a French expression used to form questions. It translates to "is it" in English. "Est" is the third-person singular present tense of "être" (to be), and "ce" means "this" or "it". Here, it is used to ask about the temperature of something, wondering if "it" is cold.</div><div><strong>froid?</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "cold". It is used here to inquire about the temperature. In French, adjectives agree in gender and number with the nouns they describe, but since it is a question asking about an unspecified object or condition, it remains in the masculine singular form by default.</div>','<h2>C’était ennuyant!</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce", which means "it" or "this" in English, and the verb "est", which is the third-person singular of the verb "être" (to be). When combined as "c’est", it translates to "it is". In "C’était", the "était" is the imperfect tense form of "être", so together "C’était" means "It was".</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being in the past.</div><div><strong>ennuyant</strong> This is the present participle of the verb "ennuyer", which means "to bore" or "to annoy". When used as an adjective, as it is here, it means "boring" or "annoying". The word agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is implied to be masculine singular here.</div>','<h2>C’était mon anniversaire.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (which means "it" or "this") and "était" (which means "was"). It stands for "It was" in English. It’s the third-person singular form of the verb "être" in the imperfect tense, used to describe a past state or condition.</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "être" (to be) in the imperfect tense. "Était" refers to a state or condition in the past and is translated as "was" in English.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "my" in English. It is used here to show ownership or relation to the speaker. It’s in the masculine singular form to match the masculine noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>anniversaire</strong> This is a French noun meaning "birthday". It’s a masculine noun and is the object of the sentence, indicating what the subject is discussing.</div>','<h2>Ce sera un bon repas.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun which can mean "this" or "that" in English. Here, it’s the subject of the sentence and represents "it" or a situation that has been mentioned or is about to be described.</div><div><strong>sera</strong> This is the future tense of the French verb "être", which means "to be". The construction "sera" indicates what will be or happen in the future. In this case, it refers to the meal that will be good.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "good". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "repas" in this case. Since "repas" is masculine and singular, the adjective is also in its masculine singular form.</div><div><strong>repas</strong> This is a French noun meaning "meal". It is masculine and singular to match with the preceding article and adjective.</div>','<h2>Ce sera un bon match.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "this" or "it" in English. It’s often used as a subject to refer to something mentioned previously or something that is about to be specified.</div><div><strong>sera</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "être" (to be) in the third-person singular. In English, it translates to "will be", referring to a future event or state.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, which corresponds to "a" or "an" in English. It’s used here with a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is an adjective meaning "good" in English. In French, adjectives agree in gender and number with the nouns they describe; in this case, "bon" is masculine singular to match "match".</div><div><strong>match</strong> This is a French noun borrowed from English, meaning "game" or "match". It’s used here to refer to a sporting event. Note that in French, the "h" is silent, and it’s pronounced more like /matʃ/.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>C’est dimanche aujourd’hui!</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is). In French, "c’est" is used to say "it is" or "this is".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this phrase, it serves to identify or define the subject "ce" (it).</div><div><strong>dimanche</strong> This is a French noun meaning "Sunday". It’s one of the seven days of the week.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "today". It indicates that the statement pertains to the current day.</div>','<h2>C’est simplement différent.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" which is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" or "it" and "est" the third-person singular present tense form of "être" meaning "is". Together "c’est" translates to "it is" or "this is".</div><div><strong>simplement</strong> This is an adverb that translates to "simply" or "just" in English. It modifies the adjective "différent" to state that something is merely different or simply different without any additional implications.</div><div><strong>différent</strong> This is an adjective that means "different" in English. It describes something that is not the same as something else. In this sentence, it is used in the masculine singular form to match the subject it describes.</div>','<h2>C’est une question de goût.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense of "être" (to be). "C’est" translates to "It is" or "This is" in English. It’s commonly used to describe situations or states of being.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>question</strong> This is a French noun meaning "question". Here, it’s used to signify the subject or topic of discussion.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that can be translated to many words in English, often "of" or "from". In this context, it’s used to link "question" to "goût", implying possession or relation ("question of taste").</div><div><strong>goût</strong> This is a French noun meaning "taste". It refers to personal preference in this phrase, similar to the English expression "a matter of taste".</div>','<h2>C’est mieux que rien!</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In English, it translates to "It’s" or "This is".</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is the comparative form of "bien" which means "good" or "well" in English. "Mieux" means "better". It’s used here to compare something that is preferable to an alternative.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a French conjunction meaning "than". It’s used in comparisons to indicate the item being compared with another.</div><div><strong>rien</strong> This is the French word for "nothing". When combined with "que", the phrase "que rien" means "than nothing", which emphasizes that while something may not be ideal, it is still better than having nothing at all.</div>','<h2>C’est cent dollars par nuit.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contracted form of "ce est", which translates to "it is" in English. Often used to define or identify something.</div><div><strong>cent</strong> This is the French number for "hundred". It indicates the quantity or the cost in this sentence.</div><div><strong>dollars</strong> This is the French term for "dollars", used here since the currency being referred to is the dollar. The word remains the same in both English and French, though the pronunciation differs.</div><div><strong>par</strong> This French preposition means "per" or "by" in English. It’s used to express the rate of something.</div><div><strong>nuit</strong> This is the French word for "night". In this context, it signals that the cost given is for each night’s stay.</div>','<h2>D’accord. C’est pour combien de temps?</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression that can be translated as "okay", "alright", or "agreed". It indicates acceptance or agreement with what has been said.</div><div><strong>C’est</strong> This contraction stands for "Cela est" or "Ceci est", which means "This is" or "It is" in English. It introduces a statement or a question pertaining to a specific subject or situation.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This French preposition translates to "for" in English. It indicates the purpose or a point in time relating to the action or question being discussed.</div><div><strong>combien de</strong> This phrase translates to "how much" or "how many" in English. It is used to ask about a quantity or a degree of something.</div><div><strong>temps?</strong> This noun means "time" in English. Combined with "combien de", the question is about the duration of time or period something relates to.</div>','<h2>C’est le dernier jour d’école.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "est" (is), the third-person singular of the verb "être", which translates to "this is/it is" in English. It’s used to indicate the state or existence of something.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French meaning "the". In this sentence, it’s used to introduce the noun phrase "dernier jour d’école", specifying a particular day we are talking about.</div><div><strong>dernier</strong> This is an adjective that means "last" in English. When placed before a noun, it describes that it is the final or most recent in a series.</div><div><strong>jour</strong> This is a noun meaning "day" in English. It is the focus of the sentence, denoting a unit of time.</div><div><strong>d’</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" (of/from) and the article "le", which contracts to "d’" before a vowel or mute h. It indicates possession or attachment, meaning "of" or "from" in English.</div><div><strong>école</strong> This is a noun which means "school" in English. In this context, it specifies the type of day being referred to, indicating that it’s a day at school.</div>','<h2>C’est un sandwich au jambon et fromage.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it translates to "It is" or "This is" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>sandwich</strong> This is a loanword from English, used in French with the same meaning. It refers to a food item commonly consisting of two slices of bread with fillings in between. In French, it’s a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This is the contraction of the preposition "à" (to) and the definite article "le" (the). When combined with food items, it often means "with" or "containing". "Au jambon et fromage" translates to "with ham and cheese".</div><div><strong>jambon</strong> This is the French word for "ham". It’s a masculine singular noun referring to a cut of meat from the thigh of the hind leg of certain animals, especially pigs.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words and phrases.</div><div><strong>fromage</strong> This is the French word for "cheese". It’s a masculine singular noun referring to the food product made from pressed curds of milk.</div>','<h2>C’est mon anniversaire aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is) in French, which together mean "it is" or "this is". It functions as the subject and verb of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be".</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "my" in English. It indicates possession or association, agreeing in gender and number with the masculine singular noun "anniversaire".</div><div><strong>anniversaire</strong> This is a noun meaning "birthday" in English. It is the thing being possessed in the context of this sentence.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is an adverb meaning "today". It indicates the time when the speaker’s birthday occurs.</div>','<h2>Au moins ça c’est bien.</h2><div><strong>Au moins</strong> This phrase translates to "at least" in English. It’s used to introduce a positive aspect of a situation, notwithstanding any negatives that may also be present.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a French demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" in English. It refers to a previously mentioned item, situation, or context.</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> A contraction of "ce" and "est", which translates to "it is" in English. "Ce" stands for "this" or "it" as a subject pronoun, and "est" is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be".</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This word can be translated as "good", "well", or "fine" in English. Here it functions as an adjective and complements "c’est" to express that something is good or satisfactory.</div>','<h2>C’est une bonne raison!</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" and "est", which in this context translates to "it" or "this" in English. It is a demonstrative pronoun and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". Here, it links the subject with the attribute "une bonne raison".</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes feminine nouns when the quantity is not specified.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "bon", which means "good" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>raison</strong> This is a feminine noun in French meaning "reason". It is the object or complement of the subject, forming the phrase "a good reason".</div>','<h2>C’est parce qu’il t’aime bien!</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. In this context, "C’est" translates to "It’s because" or "That’s because" when introducing a reason or explanation.</div><div><strong>parce que</strong> This is a conjunction meaning "because" in English. It introduces the reason or cause for something. </div><div><strong>il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause in this sentence.</div><div><strong>t’</strong> This is a contraction of "te", the informal second-person singular object pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. The apostrophe indicates that the following verb starts with a vowel or silent "h", necessitating the contraction.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like" in English. It’s used to express feelings towards someone or something.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This word is an adverb meaning "well" in English. When used with "aimer", it softens the meaning to "like" rather than "love", suggesting fondness or affection.</div>','<h2>C’est un plancher en bois massif?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (this/it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In English, it’s equivalent to "It’s" or "This is".</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>plancher</strong> This is a French noun meaning "floor" or "flooring". It is a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a French preposition often used to indicate the material of something. In this context, it means "made of" or "in".</div><div><strong>bois</strong> This is a French noun that means "wood". It is used here to indicate the material of the flooring.</div><div><strong>massif</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "solid" or "massive". When used after "bois", it refers to the wood being solid as opposed to engineered or laminate. It matches the masculine singular form of the noun it describes, in this case, "bois".</div>','<h2>C’est bon, gardez la monnaie.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (this, it) and "est" (third-person singular of the verb "être", meaning "is"). "C’est" is a commonly used French expression meaning "it is" or "that’s".</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "good". In this context, it can suggest satisfaction with the service or transaction that has just occurred.</div><div><strong>gardez</strong> This is the imperative form of the verb "garder", which means "to keep". The imperative mood is used to give commands or make requests. "Gardez" is the second-person formal or plural command form.</div><div><strong>la monnaie</strong> This phrase means "the change" as in the money returned when an item costs less than the amount of money given. "La monnaie" is a feminine noun phrase that includes the definite article "la" (the) and the noun "monnaie" (currency/change).</div>','<h2>C’est le moins que je puisse faire.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contracted form of "Ce est", which translates to "It is" in English. The contraction is mandatory before a vowel or a mute h in French. It serves as the subject of the sentence and links the subject to the attribute.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English, used here in the masculine singular form. In this context, it’s part of the expression "le moins", which means "the least".</div><div><strong>moins</strong> This is the French word for "least". When used with "le", it forms the superlative adjective "the least" and is part of the fixed expression "c’est le moins que", which translates to "it’s the least that" in English.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction or relative pronoun that often translates to "that" in English. In this case, it introduces a subordinate clause "je puisse faire".</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>puisse</strong> This is the first-person singular subjunctive present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". This mood is used to express a possibility or a hypothetical situation.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to do" or "to make" in English. In this sentence, it is used to indicate the action that the speaker is able to perform, literally translating to "do" or "make".</div>','<h2>C’est l’idée la plus stupide que j’aie entendue.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (this, it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense of "être" (to be). It typically translates to "it’s" or "this is" in English.</div><div><strong>l’idée</strong> This is a combination of the definite article "la" (which becomes "l’" before a vowel sound) and "idée", which means "idea". Together, "l’idée" means "the idea".</div><div><strong>la plus</strong> This phrase consists of the definite article "la" (the) and "plus" (most). In this context, it’s used to form the superlative, meaning "the most".</div><div><strong>stupide</strong> This is an adjective meaning "stupid" in English. It’s describing the noun "idée", and agrees with it in the feminine singular form.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a relative pronoun that means "that" in English and is used to introduce a subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>j’aie</strong> This is the first-person singular subjunctive present tense of the verb "avoir" (to have), due to the preceding expression of opinion (the idea being the most stupid one).</div><div><strong>entendue</strong> This is the past participle of "entendre" (to hear), which agrees in gender and number with "l’idée". It is used here with "avoir" to form the compound past, typically known as the present perfect tense in English. In this case, it translates to "heard".</div>','<h2>Non, ce n’est pas votre jour de chance!</h2><div><strong>Ah non!</strong> These are French exclamatory phrases expressing disappointment or disagreement. "Ah" is an interjection, and "non" means "no".</div><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" in English. In the sentence, it’s used to point to the concept of "your day of luck".</div><div><strong>n’est pas</strong> This is the negation of the verb "être" (to be) in the third-person singular. "Ne... pas" wraps around the verb to negate it, creating the meaning of "is not". The "ne" is often dropped in spoken French.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It indicates that something belongs to the person being spoken to and agrees with the noun it precedes in gender and number.</div><div><strong>jour</strong> This is a noun meaning "day" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that can have various meanings, but in this context, it is equivalent to "of" in English. It’s used here to link "jour" (day) with "chance" (luck), forming a compound noun "day of luck".</div><div><strong>chance!</strong> This noun means "luck" in English. In the sentence, it is indicative of fortune or success.</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas trop cher, je suis content.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that can mean "this" or "it". It is used here to refer to something, possibly a price or a thing, that has been previously mentioned or is understood from the context.</div><div><strong>n’est pas</strong> This is a contraction of "ne est pas", which forms the negation in French. It means "is not". "Ne" is the negation particle that surrounds the verb, and "pas" is the adverb that strengthens the negation.</div><div><strong>trop cher</strong> This phrase means "too expensive". "Trop" means "too" and "cher" means "expensive". In this context, the phrase modifies something understood in the conversation as not being excessively priced.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the second clause of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it indicates the speaker’s state of being.</div><div><strong>content</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "happy" or "pleased". It agrees with the speaker’s gender and number. In this case, it is used in the masculine singular form. If the speaker were female, it would be "contente".</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas tout à fait ce que je cherche.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, often equivalent to "this" or "it" in English. Here, it stands for the subject of the sentence being referred to.</div><div><strong>n’est pas</strong> This phrase combines "ne" and "est pas", creating the negation of the verb "to be". "Ne" is the first part of a two-part negation, and "est pas" (is not) completes it, negating the statement. In spoken French, the "ne" is frequently dropped: "c’est pas".</div><div><strong>tout à fait</strong> This phrase translates to "quite" or "exactly" in English. It is used to modify the extent to which something is true, often softening a negation.</div><div><strong>ce que</strong> This phrase translates to "what" in English and is used to introduce a relative clause. "Ce" refers to a thing or an idea, and "que" is a relative pronoun that links related parts of a sentence.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the relative clause within the sentence.</div><div><strong>cherche</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "chercher", which means "to look for" or "to seek". It indicates what the subject is trying to find or obtain.</div>','<h2>Oui, mais ce n’est pas un simple balcon: c’est une terrasse.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a phrase or clause contrasting with what has already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" or "that". It refers to a noun that has been previously mentioned or is about to be mentioned.</div><div><strong>n’est pas</strong> This is a negation formed by the word "ne" and the verb "est" (the third-person singular of "être", which means "to be"), along with the negative word "pas". Together, "n’est pas" translates to "is not" in English, negating the statement that follows.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, analogous to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>simple</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "simple" or "mere". Here, it specifies that the balcony mentioned is not just ordinary.</div><div><strong>balcon</strong> A masculine singular noun in French that translates to "balcony" in English.</div><div><strong>:</strong> The colon here is used as in English to show that what comes next will explain or elaborate on what came before.</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> It is a contraction of "ce" (this/that) and "est" (third-person singular form of "être"). "C’est" translates to "it’s" or "this is" in English.</div><div><strong>une terrasse</strong> This phrase consists of the indefinite article "une" (meaning "a" for feminine singular nouns) and "terrasse", which is a feminine singular noun meaning "terrace" in English. The sentence clarifies that it’s not just a balcony but a terrace.</div>','<h2>Vous ne devriez pas prendre des drogues, ce n’est pas sain.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can be used to address someone formally or to refer to more than one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a negation particle used in French to indicate the negative form of a verb. It precedes the verb and works in tandem with another negation word such as "pas" to negate the action of the verb.</div><div><strong>devriez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural of the conditional tense of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "should". In this sentence, it indicates a suggestion or advice.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation used in French to negate a verb, working together with "ne". It comes after the auxiliary verb and completes the negative expression, meaning "not".</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to take" in English. It follows the verb "devriez" to form a phrase meaning "should not take".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French which means "some". It’s used here before the plural noun "drogues" to refer to drugs in general.</div><div><strong>drogues</strong> This is a French noun meaning "drugs". It’s the object of the verb "prendre" in the sentence, indicating what you should not take.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" or "it". It’s used here to introduce the phrase "ce n’est pas sain".</div><div><strong>n’</strong> This is another instance of the negation particle "ne" used before a vowel or silent "h", usually contracted for ease of pronunciation.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "être", meaning "is" or "to be". In this context, it’s used with the negative particle "n’" to indicate that something "is not" the case.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> As before, this is the negation word completing the negative expression. In this phrase, it’s used to mean "is not".</div><div><strong>sain</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "healthy". In the sentence, it’s used in the negative form to indicate that taking drugs is not healthy.</div>','<h2>Est-ce vraiment nécessaire?</h2><div><strong>Est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it is part of the expression "est-ce", which is a French inversion commonly used in forming questions, equivalent to "is it" in English.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, which can mean "this" or "that" in English. Within the phrase "est-ce", it effectively serves as the subject of the inquiry, "it".</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "really" or "truly". It is used to emphasize the degree or extent of the adjective or action it modifies.</div><div><strong>nécessaire?</strong> This is an adjective meaning "necessary" in English, and it is used here to describe the implied subject "it" (ce). It ends with a question mark, indicating that the sentence is a question, asking about the necessity of something.</div>','<h2>Est-ce votre avis ou un fait?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This comes from the verb "être", meaning "to be". In this context, "est-ce" is the interrogative phrase that can be translated as "is it" in English, commonly used to form yes/no questions.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It is used to indicate that something belongs to the person being spoken to and is the formal or plural form.</div><div><strong>avis</strong> This is a noun meaning "opinion" or "view" in English. It refers to someone’s personal thoughts or beliefs about a subject.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It’s a conjunction used to offer an alternative between words or phrases.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This noun translates to "fact" in English. It refers to something that is known to be true or has actually occurred.</div>','<h2>C’était très agréable ce soir.</h2><div><strong>C’était</strong> This contraction consists of the pronoun "ce" (this) and the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". In English it corresponds to "It was".</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is the French adverb for "very". It is used to amplify the adjective that follows it.</div><div><strong>agréable</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "pleasant" or "enjoyable".</div><div><strong>ce soir</strong> This phrase combines the demonstrative adjective "ce" (this) with the noun "soir" (evening or tonight). Together, it means "this evening" or "tonight".</div>','<h2>C’était sympa! Mais j’ai raté mon vol.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "était" (was), which together mean "it was". This contraction is often used in French to make the speech flow better.</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes a past state or condition.</div><div><strong>sympa</strong> This is a French colloquial adjective derived from "sympathique" meaning "nice", "pleasant", or "enjoyable". In this context, it refers to a past event that was enjoyable.</div><div><strong>Mais</strong> This is the French word for "but". It’s a conjunction used to introduce a contrasting statement.</div><div><strong>j’ai</strong> This is the contraction of "je" (I) and "ai" (have), which is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", used here as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense.</div><div><strong>raté</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "rater", which means "to miss" or "to fail". In this context, it means "missed" as in missing a transportation connection.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective for first-person singular in French. It means "my", and it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is masculine singular in this case.</div><div><strong>vol</strong> This is the noun meaning "flight". Thus, "mon vol" is "my flight".</div>','<h2>Oui, c’était amusant! Et ton week-end?</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is a contraction of "cela était" in French, which translates to "it was" in English. "C’était" is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It refers to something that has already happened.</div><div><strong>amusant</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "fun" or "amusing". In the sentence, it’s used in the masculine singular form and agrees with the subject implied in "c’était". If the subject was feminine, it would be "amusante".</div><div><strong>Et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French that means "your" in English. It’s used in the singular form here and refers to something that belongs to the person spoken to.</div><div><strong>week-end?</strong> This is a borrowed (loaned) word from English, "weekend", which refers to the end of the week, commonly Saturday and Sunday. In French, it’s written as "week-end" and pronounced similarly to the English word. It’s often used in French without translation.</div>','<h2>Même ma mère pensait que c’était une mauvaise idée.</h2><div><strong>Même</strong> This French word means "even" in English. It is used to emphasize the subject "ma mère" (my mother) in this sentence.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective "my" in English. It indicates that the mother is related to the speaker. "Ma" is the feminine singular form to agree with "mère" (mother).</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is the French noun for "mother". It’s a feminine singular noun and is the subject of the verb "pensait".</div><div><strong>pensait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "penser", which means "to think" in English. This verb is used here to convey that the mother was of the opinion or thought in the past.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This French word is a conjunction that means "that" in English. It introduces a dependent clause "c’était une mauvaise idée" (it was a bad idea).</div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is a contraction of "cela était" and translates to "it was" in English. "C’était" is the third-person singular form of the imperfect tense of "être" (to be), here referring to the impersonal subject "it".</div><div><strong>une mauvaise</strong> This phrase combines the indefinite article "une" (a) with the feminine singular adjective "mauvaise" (bad). Together they describe the noun "idée" (idea).</div><div><strong>idée</strong> This is the French word for "idea". It is a feminine singular noun and is the object of the verb "pensait" (thought). The whole phrase "une mauvaise idée" means "a bad idea".</div>','<h2>Les enfants leur couraient après, c’était hilarant.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify which children are being talked about.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "children". It is in the plural form to match the plural article "les" and refer to more than one child.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a French indirect object pronoun meaning "to them" or "for them". In this context, it indicates towards whom the children are running; this is the third-person plural form.</div><div><strong>couraient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "courir", which means "to run". It describes a past action that was ongoing or habitual.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "after". It is used here in conjunction with "courir" to form the phrase "courir après", which means "to run after" or "to chase".</div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It is used here as a part of a descriptive clause stating "it was".</div><div><strong>hilarant</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "hilarious". It describes the situation as being very funny. It is in the masculine singular form to match the subject "c’était", which is treated as gender neutral in this structure, but follows the default masculine form.</div>','<h2>C’est dommage. À part ça, c’était un beau voyage?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of "être", which means "to be". Together, "c’est" translates to "it is" or "this is" in English.</div><div><strong>dommage</strong> This is a French noun meaning "pity" or "shame". In this context, "c’est dommage" means "it’s a pity" or "it’s a shame".</div><div><strong>À part ça</strong> This phrase translates to "apart from that" or "besides that" in English. It is used to change the subject or to ask for additional information beyond what has already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense of "être", meaning "was". It is used here to talk about something in the past, "it was".</div><div><strong>un beau</strong> This phrase consists of the indefinite article "un" (a) and the adjective "beau" (beautiful). In French, the adjective usually follows the noun it modifies, but "beau" is one of the exceptions, and it precedes the noun, agreeably changing to "un bel" before a vowel or mute h.</div><div><strong>voyage?</strong> This is a French noun meaning "trip" or "journey". In this question, it’s the object, inquiring about the nature of the trip mentioned.</div>','<h2>Oui, c’était délicieux! La cuisine française est sublime.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s used here to agree with a previous statement or answer affirmatively to a question. </div><div><strong>c’était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". The part "c’" stands for "cela" which often contracts to "c’" before a vowel or mute h and here it refers back to something previously mentioned, such as a meal that was enjoyed. "était" is the imperfect tense for "was".</div><div><strong>délicieux</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "delicious". In the sentence, it’s used in the masculine singular form, which matches the referred object or experience. If the object were feminine it would be "délicieuse".</div><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in French and translates as "the". It is used here in the feminine singular form to match the feminine noun that follows.</div><div><strong>cuisine</strong> This translates to "cuisine" or "cooking" in English. Here it refers specifically to the style of cooking, i.e., French cuisine.</div><div><strong>française</strong> This is an adjective meaning "French" in English. It is the feminine singular form to agree with the noun "cuisine".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". It links the subject "La cuisine française" to the predicate adjective "sublime".</div><div><strong>sublime</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "sublime" or "excellent". It’s used in the sentence to express a high opinion of French cuisine.</div>','<h2>C’était super! Je suis allée à la plage avec ma famille.</h2><div><strong>C’était</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "être" in the imparfait (imperfect) tense, meaning "it was". "C’était" is a contraction of "Cela était" and it’s used to describe past situations or conditions.</div><div><strong>super</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "great" or "super". It’s an informal term often used to describe something enjoyable or of high quality.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis allée</strong> This is the first-person singular form of the verb "aller" in the passé composé tense, meaning "I went". "Aller" means "to go" and when combined with "suis" (the auxiliary verb "être") it denotes a completed action. The extra "e" at the end of "allée" indicates that the speaker is female. If the speaker were male, it would be "suis allé".</div><div><strong>à la</strong> This phrase translates to "to the" in English. "À" is a preposition that indicates direction or location, and "la" is the definite article for feminine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>plage</strong> This is a French noun meaning "beach". It’s a feminine singular noun.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It is used to describe accompaniment or inclusion.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is a possessive adjective which means "my". It’s the feminine singular form to match the gender and number of the noun it modifies. If the noun were masculine or plural, it would be "mon" or "mes", respectively.</div><div><strong>famille</strong> This is a French noun meaning "family". It’s a feminine singular noun.</div>','<h2>Ce sera sur place ou à emporter?</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, which stands for "this" or "it". It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to an item or an order in this context.</div><div><strong>sera</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "être" (to be) in the third-person singular. In this sentence, it’s used to indicate what will happen with the order — whether it will be for dining in or to go.</div><div><strong>sur place</strong> This phrase translates to "on site" or "for here" in English. It is used to indicate that the food will be consumed at the establishment.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It is a conjunction used to offer a choice between two or more alternatives.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that can mean "to", "at", or "in" among other things. In this context, it is part of the phrase "à emporter" which translates to "to take away" or "to go".</div><div><strong>emporter</strong> This is a verb meaning "to take away" or "to carry out". It is used here in the infinitive form and signifies that the order can be taken out of the restaurant.</div>','<h2>Oui. Ce serait vraiment bon.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds positively to a question.</div><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun for "this" or "it". It’s used here to refer to a thing or idea that has been mentioned or is known from context.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "être", which means "to be". "Serait" is used to talk about hypothetical situations or to express politeness.</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is an adverb meaning "really" or "truly". It’s used to emphasize the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This adjective means "good" in English. It is agreeing in the masculine singular form with the noun or idea to which "ce" refers. If the subject were feminine, it would be "bonne".</div>','<h2>Oui, ce serait bien, merci.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" or "it". It’s used here to refer to the subject or situation being discussed.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the third-person conditional tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates a hypothetical or polite statement, similar to "would be" in English.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This word means "good" or "well" in English. In this context, it is an adverb qualifying the verb, suggesting that something is favorable or satisfactory.</div><div><strong>merci</strong> This is the French word for "thank you". It is expressing gratitude.</div>','<h2>Ce serait une bonne motivation pour parler français.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "this" or "it" in English. It often stands for something that has been mentioned before or is about to be mentioned.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this case, it expresses a condition or hypothesis, equivalent to "would be" in English.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is an adjective in the feminine singular form meaning "good". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes.</div><div><strong>motivation</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, equivalent to "motivation" in English. It refers to the desire or drive to do something.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition meaning "for". It is used here to indicate the purpose or reason for something.</div><div><strong>parler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to speak". In English, the equivalent is "to speak".</div><div><strong>français</strong> This word can be an adjective or a noun. Here, it acts as a noun meaning "French" in English, referring to the French language.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);